        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "@gitaarik The problem with this is if a scroll does *not* occur, then the autocmd never fires to clear `search_in_progress`. \n\nIf I perform a movement that clears `hlsearch` without scrolling, then the next scroll event will re-enable highlighting even though it shouldn't have.\n\nWhat we really need is a before and after callback on the scroll event we can disable hlsearch plugins while a scroll is in progress.  Or alternatively, restore `hlsearch` to whatever it was before a scroll began. \n\nI've tried hooking on the `WinScrolled` event to do this, but `MiniAnimateDoneScroll` doesn't _always_ fire after `WinScrolled`, I'm guessing in cases of single line movements where no animation is necessary. \n\nThe best I've got so far is to debounce my `auto_hlsearch`:\n\n```lua\n            local hlsearch_timer = vim.uv.new_timer()\n\n            vim.on_key(function(char)\n              if vim.fn.mode() == \"n\" then\n                local new_hlsearch = vim.tbl_contains({ \"<CR>\", \"n\", \"N\", \"*\", \"#\", \"?\", \"/\" }, vim.fn.keytrans(char))\n\n                if hlsearch_timer:get_due_in() ~= 0 then\n                  if new_hlsearch then\n                      hlsearch_timer:again()\n                  end\n                  return\n                elseif new_hlsearch then\n                    hlsearch_timer:start(260, 0, function() end)\n                end\n\n                if vim.opt.hlsearch:get() ~= new_hlsearch then\n                  vim.opt.hlsearch = new_hlsearch\n                end\n\n              end\n            end, vim.api.nvim_create_namespace \"auto_hlsearch\")\n```\n\nThis still has edge cases though. ",
